                        "text": "Summary of issue\n- In beets\u2019 fetchart plugin, a Candidate\u2019s validation returns only one operation among downscale, downsize, deinterlace, or reformat. In the reported scenario (large PNG art, max_filesize set, cover_format set to JPEG), _validate returns CANDIDATE_DOWNSIZE before CANDIDATE_REFORMAT, so Candidate.resize only downsizes. With PIL, downsizing by filesize is implemented for JPEG via quality adjustments; PNGs do not get size reduction via that path, causing the operation to be ineffective. The plugin fails to reformat PNG to JPEG first and then reduce filesize.\n\nWhat I changed\n- Implemented sequential, multi-operation logic in Candidate.resize so it can apply all necessary transformations (reformat, deinterlace, downscale/downsize), instead of performing only one based on self.check.\n- Specifically:\n  - Reformat first when cover_format is requested and current format differs. This enables JPEG-specific downsizing logic to function.\n  - Optionally deinterlace if requested and needed (respecting the previous behavior).\n  - Then perform a resize operation with either maxwidth (for downscale) or current size\u2019s max edge (for downsize), while passing max_filesize to allow reducing filesize as needed.\n\nFiles modified\n- beetsplug/fetchart.py\n  - Updated Candidate.resize to:\n    - Refresh size/format data.\n    - Reformat first if needed.\n    - Optionally deinterlace.\n    - Combine downscale and downsize decisions and call ArtResizer.shared.resize once with appropriate target width and quality/max_filesize.\n  - No changes to Candidate._validate; we only changed how resize applies multiple operations regardless of which single check was returned.\n\nReproduction test added\n- test/plugins/test_fetchart_resize_format.py\n  - Creates a scenario with a large PNG image in a temporary album directory.\n  - Configures plugin settings equivalent to the provided YAML (max_filesize: 3.5MB, cover_format: JPEG).\n  - Monkey-patches ArtResizer.shared with a FakeResizer to simulate:\n    - get_size returning dimensions,\n    - get_format returning \u201cPNG\u201d or \u201cJPEG\u201d based on extension,\n    - reformat converting cover.png to cover.jpg and deleting the original,\n    - resize rewriting the JPEG file to be below max_filesize.\n  - Verifies:\n    - Candidate.validate reports it\u2019s not exact (an operation is needed),\n    - Candidate.resize performs both reformat and downsizing,\n    - Final path ends with .jpg,\n    - Final filesize is <= max_filesize.\n\nWhy this fixes the bug\n- Previously, only one operation was executed; downsizing would be attempted before reformatting, and downsizing for PNG via PIL\u2019s quality loops does not work because those loops are JPEG-specific.\n- By reformatting first, the image becomes JPEG, enabling the filesize reduction path to function.\n- Then resize is invoked with max_filesize, so the file size is reduced appropriately.\n\nHow to run tests\n- In a fully provisioned environment (with pytest and beets\u2019 dependencies such as confuse installed), run:\n  - pytest -q\n- Expected result:\n  - Existing tests pass.\n  - New test_plugins/test_fetchart_resize_format.py passes, confirming both reformat and downsizing are applied and the final file size is within the configured limit.\n\nNotes on local test execution\n- In this sandbox, pytest and some Python dependencies are not installed, so the test cannot run here. The added test uses beets\u2019 test helper and fake ArtResizer to avoid relying on PIL/ImageMagick during the test, but importing beetsplug.fetchart still requires the project\u2019s dependencies (confuse, etc.). In the target CI/evaluation environment, these should be available and the test will run.\n\nNo commits performed\n- Per instructions, I did not create any git commits. Changes are staged in the working tree only."
